<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarabai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1675–1761</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Tarabai Bhosale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarabai was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> queen, the widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaram-i">Chhatrapati Rajaram</a>. After his death in 1700 she ruled as regent for her young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji-ii">Shivaji II</a> and led the Maratha resistance against the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. When Shahu was released by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1707, she contested his claim, and her court became the separate line of Kolhapur. In old age she was a powerful figure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a>, where she died in 1761.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarabai.jpg" alt="Tarabai" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baburao Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarabai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
